cenarios, this under performance of FLiBe is counteracted by the increase in TBR in the initial layers of the blanket. This results in a larger overall TBR in the higher neutron flux areas of the blanket, rather than in the low TBR production areas at the rear of the blanket. For greater spatial resolution a fifty-layer blanket was cre- ated using the same methodology, as described above. The neutron flux per unit lethargy (neutron cm−2 per unit lethargy) as a function of neutron energy (group structure CCFE-709) and layer number, are shown in figures 4(a) and (b). These figures illustrate how the neutron flux spectrum is affected by penetration depth within the blanket, indicated by the layer number, with 50 representing the final layer, or the layer fur- thest from the plasma source. A comparison is made between a solely liquid lithium blanket, figure 4(a), and a hybrid dual breeder design, figure 4(b), where lithium is contained within the first 31 layers and layer 32–50 contain FLiBe. This was chosen because it most closely represents the best performing configuration in figure 2.

